// The Breakdown table (D10), p.49, keyed by Breakdown.
{
    PanicAttack: "You are overwhelmed. You are Stunned (1), unable to act, speak, or defend yourself as you are consumed by terror. After this round, you gain a -1 Burden on all checks for the next 24 hours.",
    ViolentOutburst: "You lash out in rage and fear at the nearest creature, whether friend or foe. You must make a single melee attack against them. You then immediately snap out of it, horrified by your actions.",
    DesperateFlight: "Your survival instincts take over completely. You must immediately move your full speed away from the source of your Anguish. You will not stop until you are out of sight of the horror or are physically restrained.",
    CompulsiveLie: "You refuse to accept what you've seen. You begin to rationalize the horror, creating a desperate, elaborate lie to explain it away. You must try to convince the other players of this lie, and cannot be persuaded otherwise.",
    Fixation: "You become fixated on a single, minute detail of the horror. The pattern on a Penitent's robe, the color of a Flesh-beast's eyes, a single note in the Choir's song. You are fascinated and can do nothing but stare until you are snapped out of it by taking damage or by an ally's successful interaction.",
    NewPhobia: "The source of your Anguish becomes a new, permanent phobia. Whenever you encounter a similar creature or situation, all your checks are done with Disadvantage while around it. This can only be removed through significant character development.",
    SinnersResolve: "You retreat into the bedrock of your soul: your defining Sin. For the next 24 hours, you must roleplay an extreme version of that Sin (Pride becomes arrogance, Wrath becomes blind fury, etc.). However, you become immune to Frightened and gain a temporary Advantage on all checks related to directly confronting the source of your Anguish.",
    Catatonia: "Your mind simply shuts down. You collapse, conscious but completely unresponsive. You can hear and see, but cannot move or speak. This lasts until the end of the scene or until an ally spends a full round trying to bring you back to your senses.",
    TheVoiceWithin: "You don't just hear the Choir's song; you begin to understand a piece of it. A terrible, divine \"truth\" is whispered into your mind. The GM gives you a piece of secret, dangerous information, or use the Action & Theme tables (pages 87-88) to find out what it is, but you also gain a permanent point of Anguish that cannot be removed.",
    MomentOfClarity: "The world snaps into focus with horrifying clarity. You see the Choir and the Flesh not as chaos, but as part of a terrible, cosmic pattern. You have two choices: Embrace it (You gain a Blessing of the Flesh, or a Prayer is immediately resolved) or Defy it (You immediately lose all Anguish, but the next time you would gain Anguish, you gain double instead).",
}
